FIRESTORE RULES FOR THE NEW "liquidations" COLLECTION
======================================================
The liquidation feature stores its data in a new collection called "liquidations"
(one document per PCV, document id = PCV number, e.g. PCV-2026-0001).

Firebase blocks any collection that has no rule, so add the block below to your
existing rules (Firebase Console > Firestore Database > Rules), INSIDE the
"match /databases/{database}/documents { ... }" block.

It uses the same role check your other collections most likely use. If your rules
already have a helper such as isAccounting(), use that instead of the one below.

    function lqRole() {
      return get(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(request.auth.uid)).data.role;
    }
    function lqStaff() { return request.auth != null && lqRole() in ['accounting','admin']; }

    match /liquidations/{pcv} {
      // Accounting/Admin see all; a supervisor sees only their own.
      allow read:   if lqStaff() || (request.auth != null && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid);

      // Created automatically when cash is released (by Accounting), or the first time
      // the owner opens the form for a voucher that was released before this update.
      allow create: if lqStaff() || (request.auth != null && request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid
                                     && request.resource.data.status == 'OPEN');

      // Owner may edit only while OPEN or RETURNED, and may not change ownership or
      // jump straight to VERIFIED. Accounting/Admin can verify or return.
      allow update: if lqStaff()
                    || (request.auth != null && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid
                        && resource.data.status in ['OPEN','RETURNED']
                        && request.resource.data.uid == resource.data.uid
                        && request.resource.data.status in ['OPEN','RETURNED','SUBMITTED']);
      allow delete: if false;
    }

Note: a supervisor needs read access to their own documents through a query
filtered by uid (the app already does this: where('uid','==',uid)).
